speechtext:
Please do not accuse me of referring to the Walter Scott who supports the right hon. gentleman.
69}
Sir WILFRID LAURI3R. The hon. gentleman asks me my reference, and I shall give it to him at once. If he will look at the work which has been published recently by Mr. Hutcheson, if I remember right, a new series of letters addressed by Sir Walter Scott, he will find a letter written about 1808 or 1809, in which Sir Walter Scott speaks of having been made a director of an insurance company and of having been called to take part in the proceedings of the company. Some of the members of the board asked to have the proceedings published, to which Sir Walter Scott objected, and in this letter lie speaks of ' monied men and ownied men,' and by ' ownied ' he meant in the sense of their being landed proprietors.
